# ============================================================================
# CODEOWNERS
# ============================================================================
#
# READ THIS BEFORE YOU TRUST A SINGLE LINE BELOW.
#
# 1. EVERY HANDLE IN THIS FILE IS A PLACEHOLDER.
#
#    `@<your-org>/backend` is not a team. It does not exist. Until you replace
#    these with real teams or users that have WRITE access to this repository,
#    this file is inert — it assigns nobody, requests nothing, and blocks
#    nothing. It looks like governance and provides none.
#
# 2. GITHUB WILL NOT TELL YOU THAT.
#
#    A handle that does not exist, a team that is not in this repository's
#    organisation, or a user without write access is silently ignored. No
#    warning, no annotation, no failing check. The rule simply does nothing and
#    the pull request opens with no reviewer requested. Verify your edits by
#    opening a test pull request against each path and confirming the expected
#    reviewer is actually requested.
#
# 3. THIS FILE IS ONLY AN ENFORCEMENT MECHANISM IF BRANCH PROTECTION SAYS SO.
#
#    On its own, CODEOWNERS requests reviews. It does not require them. To make
#    it a gate, enable Settings -> Branches -> branch protection rule for `main`
#    -> "Require a pull request before merging" -> "Require review from Code
#    Owners". Without that, an owner can be requested and the pull request can
#    still merge without them.
#
# 4. THE LAST MATCHING PATTERN WINS. THIS IS THE OPPOSITE OF .gitignore.
#
#    This is the single most common mistake with CODEOWNERS. In `.gitignore`,
#    later patterns refine earlier ones and both participate. Here, only ONE
#    pattern applies to any given file: the last one in this file that matches
#    it. Nothing accumulates.
#
#    So a catch-all `*` placed at the BOTTOM overrides everything above it and
#    makes the whole file pointless. The catch-all in this file is therefore
#    FIRST, and every specific rule comes after it. Add new rules below the
#    existing ones, from least specific to most specific, and keep the
#    catch-all where it is.
#
# 5. SYNTAX NOTES THAT COST PEOPLE AN AFTERNOON.
#
#    - Paths are matched from the repository root when they start with `/`.
#      Without a leading `/`, `docs/` matches a `docs/` directory at any depth.
#      Prefer the leading slash: it means what it looks like it means.
#    - A trailing `/` matches the directory and everything under it.
#    - There is no negation. `!pattern` is not supported; there is no way to
#      say "everyone except". Express exceptions as a later, more specific rule.
#    - Comments must be on their own line. A trailing comment after a rule is
#      parsed as part of the rule and breaks it.
#
# ============================================================================


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Catch-all. FIRST, deliberately — see note 4 above.
#
# Everything not claimed by a more specific rule below falls here. This should
# be a group broad enough that nothing is ever orphaned, not the one person who
# happens to know the most.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*                       @<your-org>/maintainers


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backend — .NET 10, Clean Architecture, EF Core, MSSQL.
#
# Layer boundaries here are enforced by the architecture test suite, but a test
# only proves the dependency direction. Whether a rule belongs in Domain or
# Application is a judgement call, and it wants a reviewer who holds the whole
# shape of the solution in their head.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/src/backend/           @<your-org>/backend


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Frontend — Angular, Nx, PrimeNG.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/src/frontend/          @<your-org>/frontend


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Infrastructure as code.
#
# Split by cloud because the reviewing skill is different. Terraform state
# handling and Bicep deployment scoping fail in different ways, and a reviewer
# fluent in one is not automatically useful on the other.
#
# Anything here is one `apply` away from being real, which is reason enough for
# a named owner.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/infra/gcp/             @<your-org>/platform
/infra/azure/           @<your-org>/platform


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI, deployment, and the security metadata in this directory.
#
# Everything in `/.github/` changes the guardrails for EVERY contributor at
# once. A workflow edit can disable a gate for the whole repository, and it
# does it quietly — a removed job does not fail, it simply stops appearing.
# Loosening a gate should cost a conversation with someone who knows why the
# gate is there, which is exactly what a code owner is.
#
# This includes the file you are reading. A pull request that edits CODEOWNERS
# is a pull request that edits who has to approve pull requests.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.github/               @<your-org>/platform @<your-org>/maintainers


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The agentic harness.
#
# `/.claude/` is committed rather than gitignored on purpose, and it carries
# the deterministic controls: hooks that refuse dangerous shell commands,
# protect sensitive files, scan for secrets, and block a push on a red quality
# gate — plus the permission policy and the standards every agent reads.
#
# Weakening a hook is indistinguishable from a routine edit in a diff, and its
# effect is repository-wide and permanent until someone notices. That is the
# whole argument for a dedicated owner. Treat a change here with the same
# suspicion as a change to a CI gate, because it is the same kind of change.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.claude/               @<your-org>/maintainers


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Architecture decision records.
#
# An ADR is a commitment that outlives the pull request that carried it, and an
# accepted one is never edited to reflect a new decision — it is superseded by
# a new record. See docs/adr/README.md. That convention only survives if
# somebody who knows it reviews changes to this directory.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/docs/adr/              @<your-org>/maintainers
